diagnosis_text:
The reported outage is not present and leaf_router_0_1’s uplinks to the spines are healthy. End-to-end reachability from pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) to pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) is successful in both directions (0% loss, ~1 ms RTT). On the implicated device leaf_router_0_1, both spine-facing interfaces are up: eth0 (172.16.0.7/31) and eth1 (172.16.0.11/31) show LOWER_UP, and ethtool reports “Link detected: yes” on both. BGP on leaf_router_0_1 has two established neighbors—172.16.0.6 (AS 65100) and 172.16.0.10 (AS 65101)—each receiving one prefix and advertising two, with an active BGP-learned route to 10.0.0.0/24 via 172.16.0.6. The spines corroborate this: spine_router_0_0 sees neighbor 172.16.0.7 up and has 10.0.1.0/24 via 172.16.0.7; spine_router_0_1 sees neighbor 172.16.0.11 up and has 10.0.1.0/24 via 172.16.0.11. This explains why traffic to 10.0.1.0/24 is being forwarded correctly—the fabric is intact, and routing is converged.

Localization: leaf_router_0_1, interfaces eth0/eth1; BGP sessions to both spines are established. Mechanism: healthy physical links and BGP sessions ensure correct route propagation and connectivity.

Root cause: No current fault; the issue was likely transient or a stale report.

Corrective fix: No restart is needed. If an intermittent issue is suspected, review interface and FRR logs for flaps and consider enabling BFD for faster detection; otherwise, close the ticket as resolved. 
